<font face="Arial" size="+1"><B>An Interview with Damion "Ubiq" Schubert</B></font><br>
		<font face="Verdana" size="-2" color="Black">By Jonric</font><p></p>


<font face="Arial" size="-1">In 1997, Origin Systems' Ultima Online launched commercially.  Almost instantly, it became a landmark title for the industry, effectively re-defining the entire future of computer gaming and setting the stage for the major persistent world titles that have followed as well as those still to come.  Last September, the company officially announced a very exciting project, <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/main.html" target="_new">Ultima Online 2</a>, the follow-up to its pioneering predecessor, thereby becoming the first developer to make known its plans for a second generation title.
<p>
Naturally, we've been keeping close tabs on the game ever since, and have seen that the development team shares a tremendous level of excitement and enthusiasm about it.  This has, of course, further piqued our own interest in learning more.  With the E3 trade show looming in less than two months, we weren't sure how much information about Ultima Online 2 would be available in the interim.  Fortunately, Origin was kind enough to offer us the opportunity to find out more about it in an interview with Lead Designer Damion "Ubiq" Schubert.
<p>
<b>Jonric</b>: Let's start with the overall concept for Ultima Online 2.  How would you summarize it in about 100 words or less?
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: Official spiel:  Ultima Online 2 is the pinnacle of Massively Multiplayer Game Design.  Players will be given the opportunity to tailor their characters, both visually and statistically, to an extreme degree, and then be given the opportunity to take those characters into a rich, vivid, and unique world and experience the best massively multiplayer adventuring the world has ever known!
<p>
On a more personal level, UO2 is going to be the best thing coming when it comes to doing the things we really like doing in MMPRPGs, be it role-playing, adventuring, socializing, or going mano a mano with other players.  On top of it all, we are designing the game systems from the outset to be fast, stable and easily expandable.  When I look at what we're doing here, well.... there's no other way to describe it other than to say that it's the next generation of persistent world gaming.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: When did the idea to create Ultima Online 2 first arise, and when did work actually begin?  
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: Once Ultima Online exceeded Origin's wildest expectations, it was not terribly difficult to convince people that maybe we should aim even higher. Preliminary work on the design of Ultima Online 2 began in spring of 1998, and we began building the team in earnest in fall of that year.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: The team that has been assembled is a very accomplished one, but I suspect many of our readers may not yet be familiar with the key people.  Who are the leads on the team, and can you tell us a bit about their industry backgrounds?
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: The team is led by:<br>
* Jeff Anderson (Executive Producer) - Former Executive Producer of UO: The Second Age.<br>
* Starr Long (Producer) - Former Director of Ultima Online.<br>
* Jeremy Gaffney (Lead Programmer) - Founder and former Lead Programmer of Asheron's Call.<br>
* Victor Meinert (Art Director) - Key member of the art team responsible for some of the amazing visuals in Ultima Ascension. <br>
* Andy Hoyos (Creative Director) - Former Art Director for King's Quest V & Phantasmagoria, as well as a multitude of other Sierra titles.<br>
* Damion Schubert (Lead Designer) - Former Lead Designer of Meridian 59.<br>
* Ben Hanson (Lead Designer, UO2 Live Team) - Former member of the GemStone III team, as well as Lead Designer of Cyberstrike II, and a Lead Designer of DragonRealms.<br>
* Mark Vittek (Lead World Builder) - Former Lead Designer for the successful Crusader series.<br>
* Jay Lee (Lead Game Programmer) - Former AI Programmer of Sierra's SWAT 2, and Lead Programmer of Sierra's Navy Seals.<br>
* Kay Gilmore - (Director of Development) - Former Director of Origin QA, including Ultima Online.
<p> 
However, describing the leads only points to a fraction of the maturity and expertise that we have on this team.  Someone recently pointed out that we have almost no system designers or programmers who weren't leads at other times on other projects (many of them massively multiplayer projects!).  
<p> 
I couldn't imagine building a better team to make this game.
<p> 
</font><b>Jonric</b>: It has been stated that the setting for Ultima Online 2 will be in Britannia, but on a separate time line.  How and when does this time line diverge from the stand-alone games and from Ultima Online?  
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: First, a bit of background on Ultima Online: UO takes place in a "splinter universe" that breaks off from the main single-player Ultima thread at the time of the shattering of the Gem of Immortality in the first Ultima.  However, the timeline of Ultima Online takes place considerably later than this, running somewhat concurrently with Ultima IV and V.   However, since it is a different timeline, none of the events since the first Ultima in the Ultima Prime world have occurred in Ultima Online.  Thus, the Avatar never came, the Guardian has yet to arrive, and Minax' nasty plans have taken a different path, as the players of UO are now finding out.
<p>
Ultima Online 2, in turn, splinters off of THAT timeline and takes a different path.  The cataclysm that causes the events leading to UO2 happens during UO's timeframe.  However, players will find that in UO2, 200 years have transpired since the cataclysm that caused the worlds to diverge yet again.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: What is the backstory that leads up to this divergence, and then to the beginning of the game?  
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: I would certainly hate to shortcut the excellent fiction that is being put up on the UO2 web site.  I'm afraid I am just going to have to refer you to that for the time being, and recommend that you watch it as it unfolds.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: Fair enough.  Then let me ask about some specific in-game topics.  For starters, will there be skill trees, and how will they work?  
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: Ultima Online 2 has a completely new advancement structure that encourages an incredible amount of character specialization and diversity.  In many ways, it was inspired by Ultima Online and the lessons learned from it, but in other ways it is radically different.  
<p>
The skill trees in UO2 resemble Civilization or Command and Conquer tech trees.  A skill in a skill tree is binary, meaning a player either has it or he doesn't.  That skill can either give the player an ability (i.e. the Steal ability) or improve the ability (i.e. +20% chance success to the Steal ability).  A player purchases these skills from teachers throughout the world by spending skill points which they acquire through the course of their adventures.
<p>
The advantages of a system like this can quickly become apparent if you take an example like the magic skill tree.  A mage could, for example, spend his skill points in several ways: learning new spells in his chosen school, increasing the power of the spells in his chosen school, picking up a second school of magery, or possibly even picking up some light crafting ability or improving his low level combat ability.  One can quickly see how two mages of equal skill totals might have vastly different abilities.  This is superior, in our opinion, to being locked into a single magic track or an unchangeable class.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: One of the distinguishing points of UO is the prevalence of craft and other non-combat skills.  How important will these be in UO2?
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: Players will be able to craft and harvest a variety of items, and the infusion of new technology increases the value of the tinkers of the world.  On top of that, our skill tree design helps to allow blacksmiths to specialize and differentiate themselves from each other, as well as allowing die-hard blacksmiths to become true masters of their craft.
<p>
Additionally, we've been working hard to ensure that the desire for crafted goods will always exist.  The key rule of any economy is supply and demand, and thus, if you put craftsmen in the game, you have to ensure that demand for their goods exists.  We will extrapolate on those plans later. 
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: Another question with its roots in UO - will it be possible to achieve absolute mastery in different types of skills and to become, to use a classic example, tank-mages?  
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: In the skill system we've devised, players should be unable to master all of the magic skills on their own, much less be master of both magic and combat!  It will take a LOT of skill points to become a master of any one school of magic, or any one school of combat. 
<p>
Don't get me wrong - players will be able to create warrior-mage characters.  However, these characters will find that their individual skills are nowhere near those of someone who dedicated all of his/her learning towards one or the other.
<p>
We seriously wanted to create a system that encourages specialization and promotes individuality.  We wanted a system that was virtually macro-proof.  We wanted a system that allowed players to 'back up' if they made a decision they didn't like. Given the preliminary tests that we've run, we feel confident that we will achieve our goals.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: An element that is getting a lot of attention these days, especially in terms of creating believable game worlds, is AI.  What are your major goals in this area?
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: Our primary goal is to provide monster AI that is both incredibly immersive as well as being a worthy strategic challenge to the adventurers that will frequent our world.  Our long-term goals include monsters using their abilities intelligently, monsters that aren't exploited easily, monsters that target intelligently and monsters that work in groups tactically.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: Sounds cool.  Before we finish, do you have anything you'd like to tell our readers, or ask them?
<p>
<font color=#000068><b>Ubiq</b>: Working on the Ultima Online 2 project has been the single greatest pleasure and honor of my life.  I feel an enormous responsibility in following in the footsteps of Ultima Online, which I firmly and honestly believe is one of the most ambitious and significant games to ever be released.  I will do my best to live up to the expectations laid before me.
<p>
To do so, though, I need your help.  I'm a smart guy, and my team is phenomenal, but there is no comparison to the massive ocean of ideas and experience that I know lurks out there.  The UO2 team reads the various dev boards regularly, and is continually trying to find new ways to solicit input from the fans that are out there.  We need you guys to come to these sites, and offer your ideas, to flesh out our designs with your wants and needs and to polish and balance game systems as we go through beta.  We are only crafting the world.  You will be the ones that live there.
<p>
</font><b>Jonric</b>: Our sincere thanks to Damion and to Origin Systems for the chance to learn more about this exciting new game that thousands of fans are already awaiting with great anticipation.  For further news and information as development moves forward over the coming months, we invite you to stay tuned right here at our new Ultima Online 2 Vault, and to post your questions, comments and suggestions on our <a class=post href="http://vnboards.ign.com/default.asp?cat=5030" target="_new">Ultima Online 2 boards</a>. 
<p>
Jonric - Richard Aihoshi
</font>


</td></tr></table>

